  "examples": [
    {
      "title": "2-layer board, fab only, mostly done",
      "input": {
        "layers": "2",
        "asm": false,
        "panel": false,
        "l_cu": true,
        "l_mask": true,
        "l_silk": true,
        "l_outline": true,
        "l_cutouts": true,
        "d_pth": true,
        "d_npth": true,
        "d_slots": true,
        "d_units": true,
        "f_x2": true,
        "f_origin": true,
        "n_material": true,
        "n_copper": true,
        "n_finish": true,
        "v_view": true
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "4-layer board with assembly and a panel, just started",
      "input": {
        "layers": "4",
        "asm": true,
        "panel": true,
        "l_cu": true,
        "l_inner": true,
        "l_outline": true,
        "d_pth": true
      }
    }
  ],
  "usage": "Give the layer count (2, 4 or 6), whether assembly is ordered (asm) and whether you supply the panel (panel), then true for every item that is done, by key (l_cu, l_outline, d_pth, d_npth, f_origin, n_finish, a_cpl...). It returns the progress, the open items with why each matters, a per-group summary and an 'Open items' text for a hand-off. Use it right before sending a board to the fab.",
  "sources": [
    "Ucamco Gerber Layer Format Specification (RS-274X / X2, .gbrjob job file)",
    "IPC-D-356 bare-board electrical test data format",
    "IPC-6012 performance classes",
    "Common fab order-hold reasons: rule of thumb"
  ]
